Aquaman has netted a gigantic haul at  the box office with audiences and  critics enjoying the stunning deep-sea  adventure but how does the half human  half Atlantean superhero measure up  against DC's previous critical and  commercial sensation Wonder Woman  yippee-kay-yay be lovers I'm Jan and in  this video and comparing DC's two most  recent standalone origin movies /  Aquaman and Wonder Woman  keep watching to the end where I'll  reveal the same animated Easter egg that  was snuck into both movies and if you've  got any other upcoming movie should like  me to compare let me know in the  comments below this is one thing Aquaman  and Wonder Woman both succeed at its the  impressive scale of their world building  in Aquaman we get to visit all of the  Seven Kingdoms by the end of the movie  so there's plenty of amazing underwater  spectacle what's great about Aquaman is  that unlike some of DC's previous  cinematic releases it feels like  director James Wan has been given much  more free rein to indulge his  imagination without too much studio  meddling and the scale of the world  building makes me think of epic fantasy  flicks like Lord of the Rings which is  fitting because according to e w1  considers Aquaman a fantasy film not a  superhero movie so when we enter  Atlantis for the first time the visuals  are simply breathtaking going to give  props for that to the film's production  designer art department and VFX teams  there are plenty of arresting sites  throughout the movie and just when you  think it can't get any more insane it  does it's like a weird fever dream of a  film with so much to look at on screen  that at times you feel like you can't  quite take it all in while the movie  clearly lays out its own individual  world it's impossible not to make some  comparisons with other fantasy and  sci-fi worlds like Star Wars which one  cites as a major influence and  Atlantis's gateway bridge and border  control remind me of Asgard Bifrost  Bridge in the Thor movies and the light  colorful tone of Aquaman isn't miles  away from Thor Ragnarok the extensive  travels we go on in Aquaman mean we get  quite a mix of genres which I'll talk  about shortly and they also set up some  cool moments like the multiple wall  smashing action in Sicily and the  monstrous portrait like shot of Arthur  and Mara diving into the trench with a  littler and  then there's a crazy hidden Kingdom full  of dinosaurs at the center of the earth  which although inspired by comics in the  film feels almost like Aquaman as  briefly crossed over time and space and  landed in Jurassic Park when the woman's  world building is let's be honest a good  bit more restrained than Aquaman's  there's a lovely introduction to sir  mascara and the Amazons during the  scenes where we watch Diana grow up and  learn about the customs of her warrior  people though we do also get some  obvious green-screen moments such as  young Diana jumping off a wall and  Aquaman also suffers from some poor CGI  during flashbacks including the weird D  aging of Arthur's father and Willem  Dafoe's volcko in some ways it's a shame  the Wonder Woman movie doesn't spend  longer on the Paradise Island but the  story of course needs Diana to leave and  defeat Ares the God of War though the  movies design and art team certainly do  a marvelous job creating Diana's worlds  the locations we visit in Wonder Woman  are necessarily more grounded in reality  and their range isn't quite as rich as  in Aquaman where Wonder Woman did things  better is in exploring the mythology of  famous Kira and the Amazons rather than  Aquaman's somewhat brief flashback to  the formerly above Waterworld of  Atlantis Aquaman is a real whirlwind of  genres it's a bit like the filmmakers  decided to throw everything at the wall  and see what sticks there are moments  that lean heavily into horror which  isn't surprising given that the  co-creator of the sort and country  franchises is in the director's chair  there are action adventure sequences in  the vein of Indiana Jones The Mummy  movies and Romancing the stone with a  touch of The Fast and Furious franchise  thrown in remember that James Wan  directed the seventh film in that series  and the influence of Creature Features  also rears its ginormous head in fact  Wallace talked of Aquaman being a horror  monster movie on top of that as a ton of  Star Wars esque moments as well as  fantastical children's stories and  Arthurian legends thrown into the bland  some of these inspirations gel together  better than others personally I love the  horror heavy trench sequence when it  comes to Aquaman's tone though you could  certainly never accuse the movie of  taking itself too seriously which feels  about right given the characters history  director one isn't afraid to pile on the  cheese and an all-out efforts  the kind of humorous moments found in  old-school action flicks so as well as  bucketfuls of action and a lesson subtle  ecological message we also get a  boatload of jokes which depending on  what makes you laugh you'll either find  funny groan worthy or a mix of both come  aboard for me the humor in window woman  manages to find its target and lands  more often than not giving it an overall  better hit rate than Aquaman the  interaction between Diana who's never  seen a man before and Steve Trevor leads  to some truly funny moments as does  Diana's innocence when it comes to life  outside the mascara and how she just to  the new world like Aquaman Wonder Woman  also blends a variety of genres taking  us some swords and sandals territory  through screwball comedy to a war drama  with added elements of spy thriller and  finishing with the obligatory big  superhero battle compared to how well so  much of the rest of the movie works  thanks in no small part to compelling  performances from the lead and  supporting cast the finale feels more  generic even if it does give us some  memorable moments as for its villains  Wonder Woman isn't as wondrous as it  could be since its baddies are drawn  rather broadly so not especially  compelling Danny Houston's Ludendorff is  largely forgettable while Elena and I  are mix a bit more of an impression as  dr. Maru aka dr. Poisson given the  story's twists and turns David Thewlis  gets a few more shades to play with  though ultimately were still left with a  pretty typical CGI filled and battle in  Aquaman the villainy is really personal  from the get-go the main antagonist is  Arthur's half-brother orme  and the movie's secondary antagonist  David Kane aka Black Manta also has a  personal family based beef with Aquaman  Patrick Wilson's arm gets more screen  time than the individual villains in  Wonder Woman so in theory he gets the  opportunity to make more of an  impression but in reality like many of  the characters in Aquaman all has a heck  of a lot of not very scintillating  exposition to deliver in his dialogue  still opposite Jason mamoa is  hard-drinking tough but kill vipers  Aquaman the fact that Wilson plays armed  with all the gravitas and  self-importance for character straight  out of a Shakespeare drama seems  strangely fitting as for Black Manta we  only get so much of him and his  comic-book accurate costume this time  and he's basically there to be  introduced and built up as an important  future fo the Apfel man movie certainly  doesn't skimp on fights or battle scenes  showing us a vast range of  confrontations both below water and on  land including submarines taken over by  pirates gladiatorial style challenges  featuring a drum playing octopus  weaponized wine bottles storm-tossed  ships and deep sea dives as well as a  humongous sea monster voiced by Julie  Andrews and that's all before we even  get the final big underwater battle  during that climactic clash of the  undersea Titans we hear a character say  we are going to give them a fight that  they'll never forget which feels like a  lot of pressure to put on the movies  finale but there are definitely some  extremely eye-popping visuals in this  sequence there's after rising up out of  the ocean floor astride the Khorasan on  riding a masa saw that chomps up a  colossal crustacean and then there's an  army of laser shooting sharks plus a  huge array of other crazy creatures  basically Aquaman doesn't just embrace  excessive positively rebels in it it's  like the film is asking the question why  have less when you can have more more  more as much fun as this is it still  falls into the same trap of many other  such films by going on a touch too long  in the final stretch especially as the  scenes are almost entirely CGI and the  longer that's on-screen the more it  starts to look noticeable sadly Wonder  Woman is also guilty of that overly long  final CGI showdown with some ropey VFX  when Diana goes up against Ares however  there are some really cool fight scenes  in Wonder Woman that are especially  memorable for their dramatic settings  the Superman inspired alleyway fight  were Diana save Steve with her bracelets  is a delightful scene and there are some  beautiful balletic martial art style  moments in the battle between the German  soldiers and the Amazons even though  some of the effects may be flawed but  Diana striding out onto no-man's land is  the movie standout action scene nothing  in Aquaman is quite as visceral moving  and or inspiring as this moment and the  absolutely mind-blowing the amazing  thing about that scene is that it was  very nerve  from the movie as initially the studio  executives couldn't get their heads  around who diana was meant to be  fighting in the scene as it's not about  just one specific big bad now you know I  love my superhero movies but one thing  about though I'm not so keen on is their  romantic subplots because they often  feel shoehorned into the story and the  romantic interest frequently have less  than interesting roles but not a lot to  do other than be saved by and swoon over  the heroic lead while amber herds Mara  does get some cool moments in the  Aquaman movie holding back waves and  kicking some ass a big part of her role  is to relay information to Arthur and by  extension to us in the audience well  though mare is engaged to a Thurs  half-brother norm most of her scenes are  alongside Jason Momoa so it's  unfortunate that Momoa and herds  on-screen chemistry isn't exactly  sizzling it's not like the zero spark  between them it's more that their  eventual kiss doesn't really feel  learned but rather simply inevitable  because that's what happens in scripts  like this and that's why the way their  relationships depicted can't compare to  Diana and Steve and Wonder Woman  thanks to gal gadot and Chris Pines line  delivery and obvious chemistry patty  Jenkins direction and Allan Heinberg  script  Wonder Woman takes small quiet moments  like Diana and Steve's boat journey or  dancing together after saving a war-torn  village and turns them into humorous or  touching scenes that reveal a lot to us  about them as individuals and potential  partners so when Steve tells Diana he  loves her in the final act it's not a  throwaway moment that's inevitable  because that's what these movies do  instead the script has work to build  their characters to this point and  earned that moment much like her fellow  sea-based redhead Ariel in the Little  Mermaid Mara gets several literal  fish-out-of-water moments where she's  faced with a strange new world the  surface dwellers curiously Donna has her  own Ariel moment in Wonder Woman when  she drags Steve Trevor from the sea in a  scene that clearly pays homage to  Disney's animated feature okay now it's  your turn to decide whether you think  Aquaman or Wonder Woman is the better  movie I've added a poll where you can  vote just tap in the top right of this  video and after you've cast your vote  you'll be able to see the results let me  know what you think in the comments for  and if you enjoyed 